{"description":"Trump asserts a former president admitted regret over not attacking Iran, yet the former presidents deny recent contact.","articleSummary":"President Donald Trump repeatedly claimed that he had elicited a confession from a former U.S. president who expressed regret for not acting against Iran, stating the conversation took place in a private setting. Trump withheld the identity of the former president, citing concerns over embarrassing the individual, and said he had spoken to the ex‑president in a meeting he held at the White House while opening a Kennedy Center trustees’ session. Representatives for all four living former presidents—Bill Clinton, George W. Bush, Barack Obama and Joe Biden—responded that none had been in contact with Trump in recent times, and the White House declined to comment. Trump used the occasion to reaffirm his hard‑line stance on Iran, claiming no previous president had the courage to confront the threat, while also praising Clinton and criticizing Biden and Obama for their perceived weakness on the issue.

The story also noted Trump’s broader political attacks on Biden and Obama, labeling Biden the “worst president” and accusing Obama of negotiating a “horrible deal” with Iran, a criticism he had levied during his first term. He announced a task force to eliminate fraud in federal benefit programs, led by Vice President JD Vance, in a speech held in the Oval Office.

The article reports the factual claims made by Trump and the responses from former presidents’ representatives, highlighting a discrepancy between the president’s statements and the denials from the former leaders’ camps.","points":["Trump said he spoke to a former president who regretted not attacking Iran.","Trump declined to name the former president, claiming he didn’t want to embarrass him.","Representatives for Bill Clinton, George W. Bush, Barack Obama and Joe Biden said they had no recent contact with Trump.","Trump praised Bill Clinton and criticized Biden and Obama over the Iran deal.","The White House did not comment on the claim of contact between Trump and a former president."],"biasSummary":"The article presents Trump’s claims alongside the denials from former presidents’ representatives, suggesting a tone of skepticism toward Trump’s assertions. By repeating Trump’s criticism of Biden and Obama while briefly noting his favorable remarks about Clinton, the piece highlights Trump’s partisan rhetoric, potentially framing his statements as politically motivated rather than substantiated."}
